┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── XRPL Camp — try ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Breaking it on purpose                                                                                                                  │
│                                                                                                                                         │
│ Every one of these runs against the live network and none of them                                                                       │
│ signs anything, sends anything or costs a drop. The ledger tells us                                                                     │
│ exactly what it would have done.                                                                                                        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Send to an address with one character wrong
  Sending to: rp6xWefaU8zKjNqUQpsMp15WRDTYyFq6QA  (one character off)
  refused  caught by your machine, before anything is built
  An XRPL address carries its own checksum, so this never left your machine. You cannot send to an address that does not exist because you 
cannot type one by accident.

  Send a payment to yourself
  Sending to: rp6xWefaU8zKjNqUQpsMp15WRDTYyFq6QC  (yourself)
  XRPLModelException  caught by the library, before anything is signed
  The ledger would reject this as temREDUNDANT, but it never got the chance: the library refused to build the transaction at all.

  Offer a fee the network will not relay
  telCAN_NOT_QUEUE  caught by the server, before consensus
  The server would not even pass it on. A transaction rejected here never reaches consensus and never costs anything — which is why the fee
is a queue, not a charge.

  Look up a transaction that never existed
  Looking up: A4E7DBAED0492C4AD1AC518B4776205C5F3A6C5F15B57C313B951CE37AF345BE
  txnNotFound  caught by the ledger itself
  That is a perfectly well-formed hash. It is just not a transaction. The ledger does not guess, and reading is free, so asking cost 
nothing at all.

  Send the same transaction a second time
  telCAN_NOT_QUEUE  caught by the server, before consensus
  Every transaction carries a sequence number and each one can be used once. Replay a signed transaction and the ledger has already moved 
past it. This is why nobody can charge you twice.

  Send one drop to an account that does not exist
  Sending 1 drop to: rER9AEq9jvQjVhyfnNTiAWxMJ3RFqLXBp  (brand new)
  tecNO_DST_INSUF_XRP  caught by the ledger itself
  An account has to be funded to at least the base reserve before it exists at all. One drop is not enough to bring it into being — and 
this is the first failure so far that would have reached the ledger.
  For real, this one lands on the ledger and costs 10 drops. It cost 0 here.

  Spend more than you have
  Sending: 99,999,979 drops (99.999979 XRP)  (more than you have)
  tecUNFUNDED_PAYMENT  caught by the ledger itself
  Your balance is not your spendable balance. The reserve is locked for as long as the account exists, and a payment that ignores it lands 
on the ledger as a failure you paid for.
  For real, this one lands on the ledger and costs 10 drops. It cost 0 here.

┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── The receipt ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 7 deliberate failures. This run cost 0 drops.                                                                                           │
│                                                                                                                                         │
│   5 free   — refused before the ledger ever saw them                                                                                    │
│   2 costly — would have landed, 20 drops and a permanent row                                                                            │
│                                                                                                                                         │
│ The first three letters tell you which, before you press send:                                                                          │
│   tem tel tef  malformed, rejected locally, already stale — never applied, never charged                                                │
│   tec          the ledger accepted the transaction and recorded that it failed. You pay.                                                │
│   tes          success                                                                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
